From 998a91f7d277b939f8c8aa2970092265e5570a9a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Damien Dusha <Damien Dusha>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 20:52:49 +1000
Subject: [PATCH] master: Delay_Resp carries logMinDelayReqInterval, not the
 Delay_Req's 0x7F

Fixes #30. A ptp4l slave of a flexPTP master logs

    ignore bogus delay request interval 2^127

because ptp_send_delay_resp_message() builds the Delay_Resp header as a copy
of the incoming Delay_Req header and never sets logMessagePeriod. A Delay_Req
carries 0x7F there (IEEE 1588-2019 Table 42, 1588-2008 Table 24, and what
ptp4l sends), so the master echoes 0x7F back. The same table says a
Delay_Resp carries the master's logMinDelayReqInterval -- the interval the
slave should space its Delay_Reqs at -- which is this profile's
logDelayReqPeriod.

9eefd95 set flexPTP's own Delay_Req to 0x7F, which is correct for a Delay_Req
but does not touch the Delay_Resp; since it, a flexPTP master echoes 0x7F to a
flexPTP slave as well as to ptp4l.

PTP_LOGPER_SYNCMATCHED -- "send a Delay_Req after every Sync" -- is 127, the
same bit pattern as 0x7F, so copying logDelayReqPeriod through unexamined
reintroduces the bug for exactly that setting. It is mapped to the Sync
interval it means.

Measured on a NUCLEO-H563ZI as master, ptp4l 4.4 as slave on an Intel i226,
a fresh ptp4l process per case (it rate-limits the warning to once per
300 s), counting the warning:

                                  delreq period 0    delreq SYNCMATCHED
    before                               1                   1
    naive fix (copy the period)          0                   1
    this fix                             0                   0

ptp4l reaches SLAVE in every case and holds the board within about +/-20 ns;
it uses the Delay_Resp for the path delay either way. What the bogus value
costs is the slave learning the master's interval.

Separately: the `ptp period` CLI help advertises `matched`, but the parser
compares against "match"; `matched` falls through to atoi() and silently sets
the period to 0. Not changed here.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/flexptp/master.c | 15 +++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/flexptp/master.c b/src/flexptp/master.c
index e8515d0..f6c5131 100644
--- a/src/flexptp/master.c
+++ b/src/flexptp/master.c
@@ -193,6 +193,21 @@ static void ptp_send_delay_resp_message(const RawPtpMessage *pRawMsg, const PtpH
     header.clockIdentity = S.hwoptions.clockIdentity; // set our clock identity
     header.control = PTP_CON_Delay_Resp;
 
+    // logMessageInterval of a Delay_Resp is the master's logMinDelayReqInterval (IEEE 1588-2019
+    // Table 42; 1588-2008 Table 24) -- the interval the slave should space its Delay_Reqs at.
+    // The header above is a copy of the incoming Delay_Req, whose logMessageInterval is 0x7F
+    // by the same table, so without this the master echoes 0x7F back and a ptp4l slave logs
+    // "ignore bogus delay request interval 2^127".
+    //
+    // PTP_LOGPER_SYNCMATCHED is flexPTP's own "send a Delay_Req after every Sync", and it is 127
+    // -- the same bit pattern as 0x7F -- so copying logDelayReqPeriod through unexamined would
+    // reintroduce the bug for exactly that profile. The interval it means is the Sync interval.
+    int8_t logMinDelayReqInterval = S.profile.logDelayReqPeriod;
+    if (logMinDelayReqInterval == PTP_LOGPER_SYNCMATCHED) {
+        logMinDelayReqInterval = S.profile.logSyncPeriod;
+    }
+    header.logMessagePeriod = logMinDelayReqInterval;
+
     // write fields
     ptp_construct_binary_header(delRespMsg.data, &header);        // HEADER
     ptp_write_binary_timestamps(delRespMsg.data, &t4, 1);         // t4 TIMESTAMP
